The code is compiled with

make clean
make

make creates all data files and plots

The different tasks are split into different parts

./main A1
./main A2
./main B
./main C

Task A1 tests the response function of the neural network using manually chosen parameters.

Task A2 trains the network to approximate the function
cos(5x - 1) exp(-x^2)
on the interval [-1,1]. The result is written to network.data and plotted together with the training points.

Task B uses the trained network to calculate the function, its first derivative, second derivative, and antiderivative. The results are written to derivatives.data.

Task C trains the network to solve the differential equation
F''(x) + F(x) = 0
with boundary conditions F(0)=0 and F'(0)=1.
The exact solution is sin(x). The result is written to ode_network.data.

network.data contains x, exact function value, and network approximation.

training.data contains the training points used in Task A2.

derivatives.data contains x, exact function, network approximation, exact derivative, network derivative, exact second derivative, network second derivative, and network antiderivative.

ode_network.data contains x, exact sin(x), network approximation, and the differential-equation residual F''(x)+F(x).

The class to keep the neural network is taken from the assignment.
The class declaration is in neural.h and the member functions are implemented in neural.cc.

The parameter vector p stores the neuron parameters like this:
p[3*i] = a_i      // shift 
p[3*i + 1] = b_i  // scale
p[3*i + 2] = w_i  // weight

The network formula from the homework is 
Fp(x) = ∑i f((x-ai)/bi)*wi

The activation function is
f(x) = x exp(-x^2)

The first derivative, second derivative, and antiderivative of this function
are also passed to the network. These are needed for Task B, where the
network derivative and antiderivative are evaluated, and for Task C, where
the network is used to solve a differential equation involving F''(x).

more comments are in the code 

The function response(double x) evaluates the neural network.

The function train(x, y):
We want to minimize equation 3 in the lecture notes. 
The factor 1/n is omitted because multiplying by a constant does not change where the minimum is. (it only changes the size of the cost and gradient by a constant factor.)
The training function minimizes the sum of squared residuals using the Newton minimizer implemented in minimization.cc.
For Task A/B an analytical gradient of the cost function is used.

